1133
00:17:09,250 --> 00:17:13,150
Simple assumption. Chronological proximity equals spatial proximity.

1134
00:17:13,370 --> 00:17:16,370
Meaning if they were taken at the same time, they were probably taken in the same place.

1135
00:17:16,370 --> 00:17:20,930
Exactly. If you were walking around the fountain at a steady pace recording a video,

1136
00:17:21,410 --> 00:17:27,190
frame number 150 was physically captured a fraction of a second after frame 149 and just before frame 151.

1137
00:17:27,490 --> 00:17:34,530
Because of that timeline, frame 150 and 151 are basically guaranteed to share a massive percentage of their visual features.

1138
00:17:34,530 --> 00:17:40,350
They are looking at the exact same patch of concrete from maybe a millimeter apart.

1139
00:17:40,550 --> 00:17:47,390
Right. So sequential matching tells the software, hey, do not bother comparing frame 150 to frame 800.

1140
00:17:47,890 --> 00:17:51,730
Just check the five frames immediately before it and the five frames immediately after it.

1141
00:17:51,870 --> 00:17:54,990
That is brilliant. It dramatically slashes the workload.

1142
00:17:55,110 --> 00:17:57,270
The massive N squared problem just vanishes.

1143
00:17:57,610 --> 00:18:03,570
And it's replaced by a highly efficient localized search window that physically follows the path you walk.

1144
00:18:03,570 --> 00:18:04,410
Extremely fast.

1145
00:18:04,530 --> 00:18:07,630
But there is a catch with sequential matching in the real world.

1146
00:18:07,830 --> 00:18:11,070
I was just thinking about this. Let's say I'm walking around the fountain recording my video.

1147
00:18:11,390 --> 00:18:13,350
Halfway through the loop, a dog runs past me.

1148
00:18:13,590 --> 00:18:18,930
I instinctively turn around, point the camera totally away from the fountain, film the dog for 10 seconds,

1149
00:18:19,150 --> 00:18:21,510
and then turn back to the fountain to finish my loop.

1150
00:18:21,610 --> 00:18:25,150
Yeah, you have completely shattered the chronological logic of the data set.

1151
00:18:25,330 --> 00:18:30,610
Right. Frame 300 is the fountain. Frame 301 is a tree on the opposite side of the park where the dog ran.

1152
00:18:31,070 --> 00:18:32,570
Frame 600 is back to the fountain.

1153
00:18:32,570 --> 00:18:32,690
Right.

1154
00:18:34,530 --> 00:18:37,830
Frame 100 is the tree on the opposite side of the park where the dog ran.

1155
00:18:37,830 --> 00:18:44,310
And the entire 3D model will snap in half because the chain of connections is broken.

1156
00:18:44,510 --> 00:18:48,230
Yes. Frame 1 will never connect to frame 1000 because of that interruption.

1157
00:18:49,090 --> 00:18:50,890
Exhaustive matching is way too slow.

1158
00:18:51,310 --> 00:18:58,090
And sequential matching is incredibly vulnerable to interruptions or just disorganized photo collections.

1159
00:18:58,450 --> 00:19:02,090
Which is why CallMap utilizes a third highly sophisticated strategy.

1160
00:19:02,830 --> 00:19:03,990
The vocab tree.

1161
00:19:03,990 --> 00:19:04,510
The vocab tree.

1162
00:19:04,530 --> 00:19:05,970
The vocabulary tree. I love this one.

1163
00:19:06,070 --> 00:19:09,030
It sounds like natural language processing but applied to visual data.

1164
00:19:09,390 --> 00:19:13,170
The mechanism is conceptually very similar to a search engine index like Google.

1165
00:19:13,690 --> 00:19:18,650
When you select vocab tree matching, the software does a rapid preliminary scan of every image in the folder.

1166
00:19:19,210 --> 00:19:23,170
It doesn't do deep feature matching yet. Instead, it quantizes visual information.

1167
00:19:23,830 --> 00:19:28,510
It summarizes the dominant textures and shapes into a compact list of visual words.

1168
00:19:28,930 --> 00:19:31,290
So it generates an index at the back of the textbook.

1169
00:19:31,290 --> 00:19:34,290
It tags, say, image 50 with visual data.

1170
00:19:34,530 --> 00:19:38,630
Visual words like jagged stone, water ripple, and moss.

1171
00:19:38,630 --> 00:19:39,130
Exactly.

1172
00:19:39,130 --> 00:19:44,130
And when it comes time to find matches for image 50, the software just queries the index.

1173
00:19:44,530 --> 00:19:52,170
It asks the vocab tree to return a list of the top 50 other images in the entire dataset that share those specific visual words.

1174
00:19:52,250 --> 00:19:58,190
Oh wow. So it instantly filters out the 900 irrelevant photos of the sky or the pavement or that dog you filmed.

1175
00:19:58,570 --> 00:20:02,890
And it only performs the heavy intensive feature matching on the most probable candidates.

1176
00:20:02,890 --> 00:20:04,090
It is an incredibly elegant.

1177
00:20:04,530 --> 00:20:08,330
It is an incredibly elegant way to handle massive, totally disorganized ground collections.

1178
00:20:08,690 --> 00:20:09,330
It really is.

1179
00:20:09,610 --> 00:20:13,450
Now, there is actually a fourth matching option in the software called spatial matching,

1180
00:20:13,650 --> 00:20:17,050
but we need to explicitly rule it out for our specific mission today.

1181
00:20:17,190 --> 00:20:18,410
Right. Definitely rule it out.

1182
00:20:18,650 --> 00:20:22,630
Spatial matching relies on the GPS coordinates embedded in the image metadata.

1183
00:20:23,210 --> 00:20:27,090
It matches photos based on where the camera physically reported being on the globe.

1184
00:20:27,350 --> 00:20:30,070
And spatial matching is really the domain of the drone operator.

1185
00:20:30,070 --> 00:20:34,030
An enterprise drone flying in open air with an RTK module,

1186
00:20:34,530 --> 00:20:37,530
records its physical location with millimeter precision.

1187
00:20:38,050 --> 00:20:42,330
The software can safely rely on that metadata to decide which images overlap.

1188
00:20:42,450 --> 00:20:45,290
But down on the ground, GPS is a complete mess.

1189
00:20:45,370 --> 00:20:46,330
It's terrible.

1190
00:20:46,450 --> 00:20:50,610
The signal bounces off tall buildings, creating multi-path errors.

1191
00:20:50,890 --> 00:20:53,490
It gets absorbed by thick tree canopies.

1192
00:20:53,850 --> 00:20:59,450
If you rely on your smartphone's GPS chip to tell ColMap which photos of the fountain are close to each other,

1193
00:20:59,770 --> 00:21:03,650
a bad signal bounce might tell the software you were standing a hundred yards away.

1194
00:21:03,690 --> 00:21:04,490
And then the spatial matching is a mess.

1195
00:21:04,650 --> 00:21:06,410
The spatial logic just completely collapses.

1196
00:21:06,610 --> 00:21:09,250
So for capturing large outdoor spaces from the ground,

1197
00:21:09,450 --> 00:21:15,250
we must rely on the visual evidence through sequential or vocab tree matching, not the satellite data.

1198
00:21:15,450 --> 00:21:15,970
One hundred percent.

1199
00:21:16,170 --> 00:21:18,690
So once your chosen matching strategy is executed,

1200
00:21:18,890 --> 00:21:21,650
the software draws thousands of tentative connections.

1201
00:21:21,850 --> 00:21:27,210
These are literal lines linking a SIFT key point in one image to a highly similar key point in another.

1202
00:21:27,410 --> 00:21:30,170
This raw matching data is notoriously gullible.

1203
00:21:30,370 --> 00:21:32,050
Gullible is the perfect word for it.

1204
00:21:32,250 --> 00:21:33,890
The SIFT algorithm is brilliant at finding

1205
00:21:33,890 --> 00:21:38,130
high contrast, but it has zero common sense.

1206
00:21:38,330 --> 00:21:39,410
Zero.

1207
00:21:39,610 --> 00:21:44,810
Let's step away from the fountain for a second and imagine we're capturing a large historic brick building.

1208
00:21:45,010 --> 00:21:50,090
SIFT extracts thousands of features from the sharp corners of the individual bricks.

1209
00:21:50,290 --> 00:21:53,850
And the algorithm looks at a brick corner on the far left side of the building,

1210
00:21:54,050 --> 00:21:57,090
and it looks at a brick corner on the far right side of the building.

1211
00:21:57,290 --> 00:22:03,810
To SIFT, the texture, the contrast, and the local pixel neighborhood are mathematically identical.

1212
00:22:04,010 --> 00:22:10,530
So the raw matching algorithm enthusiastically draws a strong connection between the left side of the building and the right side of the building.

1213
00:22:10,730 --> 00:22:13,930
It declares then the exact same point in physical space.

1214
00:22:14,130 --> 00:22:17,810
Which is a huge problem, because if CallMap accepts that match,

1215
00:22:18,010 --> 00:22:25,290
it is going to attempt to physically fold the 3D model of the building in half just to force those two bricks to occupy the same coordinates.

1216
00:22:25,490 --> 00:22:26,290
It'll look like a taco.

1217
00:22:26,490 --> 00:22:27,690
Yeah.

1218
00:22:27,890 --> 00:22:33,690
Repetitive patterns, brickwork, modern window grids, chain link fences, even the canopy

1219
00:22:33,890 --> 00:22:38,650
and the highly textured tree will generate thousands of these false positive matches.

1220
00:22:38,850 --> 00:22:41,730
We must sanitize the data before we calculate depth.

1221
00:22:41,930 --> 00:22:45,330
And this critical cleanup phase is called geometric verification.

1222
00:22:45,530 --> 00:22:48,570
The software basically has to test the physics of the matches.

1223
00:22:48,770 --> 00:22:49,090
Right.

1224
00:22:49,290 --> 00:22:53,090
It looks at a pair of images and the hundreds of points connecting them.

1225
00:22:53,290 --> 00:22:55,490
And it uses advanced mathematical models,

1226
00:22:55,690 --> 00:22:59,890
specifically calculating what are called homographies or essential matrices.

1227
00:23:00,090 --> 00:23:02,050
While the linear algebra there is dense,

1228
00:23:02,250 --> 00:23:03,850
the underlying concept is actually

1229
00:23:04,050 --> 00:23:06,090
highly intuitive. Think about it.

1230
00:23:06,290 --> 00:23:10,890
If you take a photo of a flat wall, take one step to your right and take a second photo.

1231
00:23:11,090 --> 00:23:15,250
Every single feature on that wall should shift in your camera frame in a predictable

1232
00:23:15,450 --> 00:23:17,810
uniform direction based on your physical movement.

1233
00:23:18,010 --> 00:23:20,210
Exactly. The whole scene translates together.

1234
00:23:20,410 --> 00:23:24,690
So the geometric verification looks at the collective behavior of the entire web of points.

1235
00:23:24,890 --> 00:23:28,810
Right. So if 99 points shift uniformly to the left,

1236
00:23:29,010 --> 00:23:33,650
but one specific point like our misidentified brick corner moves to the right,

1237
00:23:33,690 --> 00:23:37,810
or jumps to a completely different area of the frame, the math flags it.

1238
00:23:38,010 --> 00:23:42,410
The software basically determines that for that specific brick to actually be

1239
00:23:42,610 --> 00:23:47,610
the same physical object, the camera would have to exist in two different locations simultaneously.

1240
00:23:47,810 --> 00:23:50,210
The movement violates the laws of physics.

1241
00:23:50,410 --> 00:23:52,570
So the software identifies the outlier,

1242
00:23:52,770 --> 00:23:55,570
severs the false connection and just deletes the match.

1243
00:23:55,770 --> 00:23:58,050
It trims the web until only the geometrically

1244
00:23:58,050 --> 00:24:00,250
consistent physically possible connections remain.

1245
00:24:00,450 --> 00:24:03,090
And with that, we have now completed feature extraction and

1246
00:24:03,090 --> 00:24:05,690
correspondence search. We finally have a pristine,

1247
00:24:05,890 --> 00:24:08,730
verified web of relationships between all our photographs.

1248
00:24:08,930 --> 00:24:12,170
But, and this is wild, we are still entirely stuck in two dimensions.

1249
00:24:12,370 --> 00:24:15,330
We haven't calculated a single millimeter of actual depth yet.

1250
00:24:15,530 --> 00:24:19,050
Nope. But we have finally reached the threshold of 3D.

1251
00:24:19,250 --> 00:24:22,970
We enter step four of the pipeline, incremental reconstruction.

1252
00:24:23,170 --> 00:24:26,290
This is where the physical world is actually born from the flat data.

1253
00:24:26,490 --> 00:24:29,970
And it requires an incredibly delicate process called initialization.

1254
00:24:30,170 --> 00:24:33,010
Initialization. So the software has to pick a starting point.

1255
00:24:33,210 --> 00:24:35,330
It has to choose two images from the folder,

1256
00:24:35,530 --> 00:24:40,850
calculate the distance to the features they share and plot the very first 3D points in the empty void.

1257
00:24:41,050 --> 00:24:41,650
Right.

1258
00:24:41,850 --> 00:24:45,810
And I think the natural assumption for anyone doing this is that the software

1259
00:24:46,010 --> 00:24:49,530
just grabs image number one and image number two and starts doing the math.

1260
00:24:49,730 --> 00:24:54,850
And that assumption is exactly why many initial attempts at 3D scanning fail.

1261
00:24:55,050 --> 00:24:56,490
For incremental reconstruction,

1262
00:24:56,690 --> 00:25:01,490
starting with two chronologically adjacent images is usually a catastrophic mistake.

1263
00:25:01,690 --> 00:25:02,690
Really? Why?

1264
00:25:03,090 --> 00:25:06,010
It all comes down to the necessity of parallax and baseline.

1265
00:25:06,210 --> 00:25:08,330
OK, let's define the baseline in camera terms.

1266
00:25:08,530 --> 00:25:11,970
The baseline is simply the physical distance between the camera's position

1267
00:25:12,170 --> 00:25:14,770
in the first photo and its position in the second photo.

1268
00:25:14,970 --> 00:25:19,490
Exactly. Now think about walking around the fountain shooting a 60 frame per second video.

1269
00:25:19,690 --> 00:25:23,970
The physical distance you travel between frame one and frame two is perhaps a fraction of an inch.

1270
00:25:24,170 --> 00:25:26,770
Oh, right. So the baseline is virtually non-existent.

1271
00:25:26,970 --> 00:25:29,290
And because the cameras are so close together,

1272
00:25:29,490 --> 00:25:31,810
the perspective shift, the parallax between the two frames,

1273
00:25:32,010 --> 00:25:33,090
is mathematically non-existent.

1274
00:25:33,290 --> 00:25:33,930
So the baseline is negligible.

1275
00:25:34,130 --> 00:25:34,930
Yep.

1276
00:25:35,130 --> 00:25:39,290
If the software tries to calculate the depth of the fountain using two viewpoints

1277
00:25:39,490 --> 00:25:42,610
that are only an inch apart, the math is incredibly weak.

1278
00:25:42,810 --> 00:25:46,090
It's kind of the equivalent of trying to accurately judge the distance of a

1279
00:25:46,090 --> 00:25:49,570
mountain peak 10 miles away by shifting your head a millimeter to the left.

1280
00:25:49,570 --> 00:25:50,810
That's a perfect analogy.

1281
00:25:51,010 --> 00:25:54,810
The resulting 3D points from that tiny baseline will be noisy,

1282
00:25:55,010 --> 00:25:58,490
wildly inaccurate and structurally unsound.

1283
00:25:58,690 --> 00:26:02,010
If you build the rest of the model on that weak foundation,

1284
00:26:02,010 --> 00:26:03,850
the entire reconstruction will fail.

1285
00:26:04,050 --> 00:26:04,970
And the software knows this.

1286
00:26:05,170 --> 00:26:06,250
During initialization,

1287
00:26:06,450 --> 00:26:09,450
CoalMaps scans the entire web of verified matches,

1288
00:26:09,650 --> 00:26:14,810
searching for a pair of images that satisfy two somewhat conflating requirements.

1289
00:26:15,010 --> 00:26:18,490
First, they must share a massive number of verified features,

1290
00:26:18,690 --> 00:26:19,730
so they're strongly linked.

1291
00:26:19,930 --> 00:26:23,730
Second, they must have a very wide physical baseline,

1292
00:26:23,930 --> 00:26:25,770
a drastic change in perspective.

1293
00:26:25,970 --> 00:26:31,050
Which brings us directly back to the specific capture strategy used by the researchers at the Oregon State Capitol.

1294
00:26:31,250 --> 00:26:31,970
When they filmed the

1295
00:26:32,170 --> 00:26:34,210
mountain, they didn't just walk in a single circle.

1296
00:26:34,410 --> 00:26:34,810
No, they didn't.

1297
00:26:35,010 --> 00:26:38,690
They walked one complete loop holding the camera high above their heads.

1298
00:26:38,890 --> 00:26:42,770
Then they lowered the camera to chest level and walked a second complete loop.

1299
00:26:42,970 --> 00:26:48,090
And that vertical shift is the absolute key to successful initialization.

1300
00:26:48,290 --> 00:26:49,730
Because of that strategy,

1301
00:26:49,930 --> 00:26:56,130
CoalMaps searches the data and totally bypasses the weak connection between frame one and frame two.

1302
00:26:56,330 --> 00:27:01,050
Instead, it might select frame one from the very beginning of the overhead loop,

1303
00:27:01,210 --> 00:27:04,050
and frame one from the very beginning of the overhead loop.

1304
00:27:04,250 --> 00:27:07,010
So, the software has a perfect stereoscopic foundation.

1305
00:27:07,210 --> 00:27:13,050
It uses that wide perspective shift to triangulate the depth of those shared features with extremely high mathematical confidence.

1306
00:27:13,250 --> 00:27:13,850
Right.

1307
00:27:14,050 --> 00:27:16,210
It plots that first cluster of images,

1308
00:27:16,410 --> 00:27:19,210
and then it takes the data from the first cluster of images,

1309
00:27:19,410 --> 00:27:22,210
and then it takes the data from the second cluster of images,

1310
00:27:22,410 --> 00:27:25,210
and then it takes the data from the third cluster of images,

1311
00:27:25,410 --> 00:27:29,210
and then it takes the data from the fourth cluster of images,

1312
00:27:29,210 --> 00:27:31,330
and then it takes the data from the fifth cluster of images.

1313
00:27:31,530 --> 00:27:32,330
Right.

1314
00:27:32,530 --> 00:27:35,130
It plots that first cluster of 3D points into the void.

1315
00:27:35,330 --> 00:27:36,610
The foundation is solidly set.

1316
00:27:36,810 --> 00:27:39,890
Now, the incremental part of the reconstruction truly begins.

1317
00:27:40,090 --> 00:27:41,450
The incremental loop. How does that work?

1318
00:27:41,650 --> 00:27:45,170
The software enters this continuous rigorous cycle.

1319
00:27:45,370 --> 00:27:48,410
It looks at the small cluster of 3D points it just generated.

1320
00:27:48,610 --> 00:27:52,570
Then it searches the remaining 2D images for a third photo

1321
00:27:52,770 --> 00:27:56,170
that shares SIFT features with those existing 3D points.

1322
00:27:56,370 --> 00:27:57,170
Okay. It finds an overlapping image.

1323
00:27:57,370 --> 00:27:59,170
Now, it has to figure out where that third camera was actually set.

1324
00:27:57,370 --> 00:27:58,170
That's right.

1325
00:27:58,330 --> 00:28:00,970
The third camera was actually standing in physical space.

1326
00:28:01,170 --> 00:28:04,530
It executes a mathematical process called image registration.

1327
00:28:04,730 --> 00:28:10,250
Yeah. And the underlying math here is known as the perspective endpoint problem, or PNP.

1328
00:28:10,450 --> 00:28:16,210
The software analyzes how the known 3D points project onto the flat 2D sensor of the new image.

1329
00:28:16,410 --> 00:28:21,250
And by reversing that projection, it calculates the exact physical XYZ coordinates

1330
00:28:21,450 --> 00:28:24,010
and the rotation of that third camera in the virtual space.

1331
00:28:24,210 --> 00:28:27,330
Once it anchors that third camera, it uses that new vantage point

1332
00:28:27,490 --> 00:28:30,130
to look at other features in the image, calculates their depth,

1333
00:28:30,330 --> 00:28:32,370
and plots even more 3D points into the void.

1334
00:28:32,570 --> 00:28:34,250
And that process is called triangulation.

1335
00:28:34,450 --> 00:28:35,250
The model grows.

1336
00:28:35,450 --> 00:28:38,730
Then it finds a fourth overlapping image, registers the fourth camera,

1337
00:28:38,930 --> 00:28:41,850
triangulates more points than a fifth, then a tenth, then a hundredth.

1338
00:28:42,050 --> 00:28:44,650
Camera by camera, point by point,

1339
00:28:44,850 --> 00:28:48,650
the geometry of the fountain is slowly pulled out of the darkness and assembled.

1340
00:28:48,850 --> 00:28:50,650
It is a phenomenal process,

1341
00:28:50,850 --> 00:28:54,130
but honestly, it immediately raises a massive red flag for me.

1342
00:28:54,250 --> 00:28:56,290
The drift. Yes, the drift.

1343
00:28:56,290 --> 00:28:58,690
If it is building this entire world sequentially,

1344
00:28:58,890 --> 00:29:03,650
if camera four derives its position from camera three and camera five derives

1345
00:29:03,850 --> 00:29:08,170
from camera four, we are essentially dealing with a mathematical telephone game.

1346
00:29:08,370 --> 00:29:13,050
And error accumulation is the single most dangerous threat to an incremental pipeline.

1347
00:29:13,250 --> 00:29:16,930
Let's say the calculation for camera three is off by just a microscopic fraction

1348
00:29:17,130 --> 00:29:20,690
of a degree due to sensor noise or a slightly blurry pixel.

1349
00:29:20,890 --> 00:29:25,450
Camera four bases its entire reality on camera three, so it inherits that tiny error,

1350
00:29:25,450 --> 00:29:29,170
and inevitably adds its own microscopic miscalculation on top.

1351
00:29:29,370 --> 00:29:29,930
Exactly.

1352
00:29:30,130 --> 00:29:33,970
And by the time the software registers camera 1000 on the opposite side of the fountain,

1353
00:29:34,170 --> 00:29:36,930
those microscopic errors have compounded exponentially.

1354
00:29:37,130 --> 00:29:38,650
The fountain would physically twist.

1355
00:29:38,850 --> 00:29:41,530
The start of the loop would never align with the end of the loop.

1356
00:29:41,730 --> 00:29:44,650
If the software merely added cameras blindly,

1357
00:29:44,850 --> 00:29:48,970
the result would always be a warped, unusable mess.

1358
00:29:49,170 --> 00:29:54,850
So to combat this drift, Colomap constantly employs a rigorous optimization algorithm

1359
00:29:55,450 --> 00:29:57,130
known as bundle adjustment.

1360
00:29:57,330 --> 00:29:58,330
Bundle adjustment.

1361
00:29:58,530 --> 00:30:03,050
The terminology in this field is so dense, but the concept is actually very physical.

1362
00:30:03,250 --> 00:30:04,930
When I first hear bundle adjustment,

1363
00:30:05,130 --> 00:30:09,450
I picture someone trying to carry a massive, overflowing armful of firewood.

1364
00:30:09,650 --> 00:30:10,450
That's a good visual.

1365
00:30:10,650 --> 00:30:15,010
A log starts slipping, the balance shifts, and the person has to momentarily stop

1366
00:30:15,210 --> 00:30:18,650
walking, shuffle the logs around, and aggressively tighten their grip

1367
00:30:18,850 --> 00:30:21,650
on the entire bundle so it doesn't all collapse onto the ground.

1368
00:30:21,850 --> 00:30:23,970
What is actually being bundled in the software?

1369
00:30:24,170 --> 00:30:25,170
The metaphor aligns perfectly

1370
00:30:25,170 --> 00:30:26,290
with the math, honestly.

1371
00:30:26,490 --> 00:30:29,890
The bundle refers to the dense web of mathematical viewing rays,

1372
00:30:30,090 --> 00:30:33,490
the invisible lines connecting every calculated camera position

1373
00:30:33,690 --> 00:30:36,050
to every triangulated 3D point in the space.

1374
00:30:36,250 --> 00:30:36,530
OK.

1375
00:30:36,730 --> 00:30:40,730
Bundle adjustment is the mathematical process of simultaneously tweaking

1376
00:30:40,930 --> 00:30:46,210
the positions of the cameras and the 3D points to minimize the tension and error in that web.

1377
00:30:46,410 --> 00:30:50,010
So the software is actively tightening its grip on the geometry,

1378
00:30:50,210 --> 00:30:54,050
and the source details two specific flavors of this optimization.

1379
00:30:54,250 --> 00:30:55,090
Local bundle

1380
00:30:55,290 --> 00:30:57,370
adjustment and global bundle adjustment.

1381
00:30:57,570 --> 00:31:00,210
Local bundle adjustment happens continuously.

1382
00:31:00,410 --> 00:31:05,410
Every single time Cullmap adds a new image and triangulates new points,

1383
00:31:05,610 --> 00:31:08,410
it pauses and runs a local adjustment.

1384
00:31:08,610 --> 00:31:11,970
But crucially, it does not recalculate the entire scene.

1385
00:31:12,170 --> 00:31:14,650
It only adjusts the firewood that is actively slipping.

1386
00:31:14,850 --> 00:31:18,410
So if the software just registered camera 800 on the north face of the fountain,

1387
00:31:18,610 --> 00:31:21,610
adjusting the cameras on the far south face won't help anything.

1388
00:31:21,810 --> 00:31:24,690
Exactly. The software isolates the immediate neighborhood,

1389
00:31:24,810 --> 00:31:27,770
the newly added camera and the cameras directly connected to it,

1390
00:31:27,970 --> 00:31:32,330
and runs a rapid optimization algorithm, usually something like Levenberg-Marcourt.

1391
00:31:32,530 --> 00:31:35,810
It subtly shifts the local points to minimize the immediate error.

1392
00:31:36,010 --> 00:31:40,250
It is a fast, efficient way to keep the local geometry tight as the model expands.

1393
00:31:40,450 --> 00:31:44,050
But local adjustments cannot solve the overarching systematic drift

1394
00:31:44,250 --> 00:31:47,210
that slowly bows the entire model over hundreds of frames.

1395
00:31:47,410 --> 00:31:51,050
Eventually, the software must reckon with the entire data set at once.

1396
00:31:51,250 --> 00:31:53,810
That is when it triggers a global bundle adjustment.

1397
00:31:53,810 --> 00:31:56,730
The software stops adding new images entirely.

1398
00:31:56,930 --> 00:32:00,690
It takes the entire bundle, every single camera pose it has calculated,

1399
00:32:00,890 --> 00:32:05,450
and hundreds of thousands of 3D points and essentially drops them all onto the floor.

1400
00:32:05,650 --> 00:32:08,050
And then it runs a massive computationally

1401
00:32:08,250 --> 00:32:11,650
punishing optimization across the entire model simultaneously,

1402
00:32:11,850 --> 00:32:16,330
forcing every single point into the most mathematically cohesive shape possible.

1403
00:32:16,530 --> 00:32:19,410
It totally eradicates the telephone game error.

1404
00:32:19,610 --> 00:32:23,610
But the source highlights a truly brilliant aspect of this global

1405
00:32:23,610 --> 00:32:28,330
optimization. The software isn't just tweaking the physical locations of the cameras.

1406
00:32:28,530 --> 00:32:33,210
It actively interrogates the camera model parameters we defined way back in step one.

1407
00:32:33,410 --> 00:32:37,610
Wait, remember the K1 and K2 radial distortion numbers we fed it for our wide angle lens?

1408
00:32:37,810 --> 00:32:40,770
Yes, exactly. During a global bundle adjustment,

1409
00:32:40,970 --> 00:32:44,890
the software looks at the massive amount of 3D evidence it's compiled and realizes,

1410
00:32:45,090 --> 00:32:48,650
hey, if I adjust my assumption about the physical curvature of the glass lens by

1411
00:32:48,850 --> 00:32:52,650
just 1%, the geometric error across the entire fountain drops dramatically.

1412
00:32:52,850 --> 00:32:53,610
That's insane.

1413
00:32:53,810 --> 00:32:58,330
The software refined its understanding of the physical lens based on the reality

1414
00:32:58,530 --> 00:33:00,010
of the 3D world it is building.

1415
00:33:00,210 --> 00:33:04,010
The model continuously teaches the software how to see it better.

1416
00:33:04,210 --> 00:33:06,730
It is a breathtaking feedback loop. It really is.

1417
00:33:06,930 --> 00:33:11,810
But this incredible, meticulous step by step process, registering a camera,

1418
00:33:12,010 --> 00:33:16,610
triangulating, local adjustment, global adjustment, leads us directly into the most

1419
00:33:16,810 --> 00:33:20,090
notorious frustration for anyone attempting 3D reconstruction.

1420
00:33:20,290 --> 00:33:21,850
It is the hardware bottleneck.

1421
00:33:22,050 --> 00:33:22,490
Yes.

1422
00:33:22,690 --> 00:33:23,530
It is the moment

1423
00:33:23,730 --> 00:33:26,810
when the theory hits the reality of the silicon sitting on your desk.

1424
00:33:27,010 --> 00:33:28,570
I see this constantly online.

1425
00:33:28,770 --> 00:33:31,370
A learner wants to reconstruct a local monument.

1426
00:33:31,570 --> 00:33:36,610
They go out by a massive top tier, incredibly expensive graphics processing unit, a GPU.

1427
00:33:36,810 --> 00:33:41,210
They load up their photos, hit run, and the feature extraction phase finishes in 90 seconds.

1428
00:33:41,410 --> 00:33:43,810
The GPU crushes the SIFT algorithm.

1429
00:33:44,010 --> 00:33:44,810
Flies right through it.

1430
00:33:45,010 --> 00:33:47,890
But then incremental reconstruction begins.

1431
00:33:48,090 --> 00:33:50,450
The progress bar crawls to a total halt.

1432
00:33:50,650 --> 00:33:53,210
The software estimates it will take five hours to finish.

1433
00:33:53,730 --> 00:33:58,570
The user checks their system monitor and their hyper expensive GPU is sitting at maybe 4% utilization,

1434
00:33:58,770 --> 00:34:01,570
while their standard CPU is maxed out and overheating.

1435
00:34:01,770 --> 00:34:06,970
Why is the actual birth of the 3D model barely using the graphics card?

1436
00:34:07,170 --> 00:34:08,690
To understand that bottleneck,

1437
00:34:08,890 --> 00:34:11,810
you really have to look at the fundamental architectural differences

1438
00:34:12,010 --> 00:34:15,130
between how a GPU and a CPU solve problems.

1439
00:34:15,330 --> 00:34:18,010
The source material provides a fantastic framework for this.

1440
00:34:18,210 --> 00:34:20,970
Think of the computational workload as a police search.

1441
00:34:21,170 --> 00:34:22,730
A GPU is structured like an army

1442
00:34:22,730 --> 00:34:24,570
of a million rookie cops.

1443
00:34:24,770 --> 00:34:27,890
Right. They have very small amounts of memory processes.

1444
00:34:28,090 --> 00:34:31,450
VRAM limitations are a massive factor in 3D processing.

1445
00:34:31,650 --> 00:34:35,410
And their internal architecture, the ALUs, are designed for very simple math.

1446
00:34:35,610 --> 00:34:38,850
So you line up your million rookies across a massive field and you tell them,

1447
00:34:39,050 --> 00:34:42,170
everyone take one step forward, stare at the single square inch of grass

1448
00:34:42,170 --> 00:34:44,290
in front of your boots and shout if you see a clue.

1449
00:34:44,490 --> 00:34:46,690
The task is massively parallelized.

1450
00:34:46,890 --> 00:34:51,850
Millions of simple independent operations executing simultaneously.

1451
00:34:51,850 --> 00:34:56,210
That is exactly why feature extraction is so incredibly fast on a GPU.

1452
00:34:56,410 --> 00:35:01,050
Finding a high contrast sift blob in the top left corner of an image has absolutely

1453
00:35:01,250 --> 00:35:04,850
zero mathematical dependency on finding a blob in the bottom right corner.

1454
00:35:05,050 --> 00:35:07,530
The million rookies scan the grid instantly,

1455
00:35:07,730 --> 00:35:09,890
but incremental reconstruction is not a search.

1456
00:35:10,090 --> 00:35:12,810
It is an unfolding, highly complex sequence.

1457
00:35:12,810 --> 00:35:14,330
You cannot parallelize a sequence.

1458
00:35:14,330 --> 00:35:16,250
You cannot register camera five until

1459
00:35:16,250 --> 00:35:19,010
the underlying math for camera four is definitively locked in.

1460
00:35:19,210 --> 00:35:21,690
And camera four depends on camera three.

1461
00:35:21,890 --> 00:35:24,370
If you have a million rookie cops to solve a sequential murder mystery,

1462
00:35:24,570 --> 00:35:26,210
the investigation collapses.

1463
00:35:26,410 --> 00:35:31,290
Rookie number 500 cannot do his job because he is waiting for rookie 499 to hand him the evidence.

1464
00:35:31,290 --> 00:35:32,530
You don't need an army of rookies.

1465
00:35:32,730 --> 00:35:34,530
You need a single master detective.

1466
00:35:34,730 --> 00:35:35,850
And that is the CPU.

1467
00:35:36,050 --> 00:35:37,570
A CPU doesn't have thousands of cores.

1468
00:35:37,770 --> 00:35:40,330
It has eight, sixteen or maybe twenty four.

1469
00:35:40,530 --> 00:35:43,370
But each of those cores is vastly more sophisticated.

1470
00:35:43,570 --> 00:35:47,210
They have access to massive amounts of system RAM and they are engineered to solve

1471
00:35:47,410 --> 00:35:51,810
intense, deeply convoluted linear algebra equations step by step without stalling.

1472
00:35:52,010 --> 00:35:54,530
So the CPU is the master detective,

1473
00:35:54,730 --> 00:35:59,130
meticulously solving the localized and global bundle adjustments,

1474
00:35:59,330 --> 00:36:02,290
unspooling the mathematical knot one camera at a time.

1475
00:36:02,490 --> 00:36:06,570
The heavy lifting of incremental 3D reconstruction is fundamentally,

1476
00:36:06,770 --> 00:36:08,810
inherently a CPU bound process.

1477
00:36:09,010 --> 00:36:11,970
You simply have to wait for the detective to finish the work.

1478
00:36:12,170 --> 00:36:15,330
That structural reality is deeply satisfying to understand,

1479
00:36:15,530 --> 00:36:19,690
but it doesn't really change the fact that five hours is a long time to wait for a model.

1480
00:36:19,730 --> 00:36:21,090
It is a very long time.

1481
00:36:21,090 --> 00:36:21,770
What if you need to

1482
00:36:21,890 --> 00:36:27,570
process thousands of ground images and you cannot afford the CPU bottleneck?

1483
00:36:27,770 --> 00:36:32,570
What if there is a way to entirely skip the slow sequential detective work?

1484
00:36:32,770 --> 00:36:36,010
Which brings us to the bleeding edge of the source material and the introduction

1485
00:36:36,210 --> 00:36:38,410
of an alternative pipeline called GLOMAP.

1486
00:36:38,610 --> 00:36:41,450
GLMAP, that stands for Global Mapper.

1487
00:36:41,650 --> 00:36:42,610
And fascinatingly,

1488
00:36:42,610 --> 00:36:45,130
Johannes Schoenberger, the original architect of the incremental

1489
00:36:45,330 --> 00:36:49,090
Coal Map, was deeply involved in developing this new approach just recently.

1490
00:36:49,290 --> 00:36:49,570
Yes.

1491
00:36:49,770 --> 00:36:51,730
He recognized the unavoidable CPU

1492
00:36:51,930 --> 00:36:55,370
bottleneck of his own software and helped engineer a fundamentally different

1493
00:36:55,570 --> 00:36:56,450
mathematical strategy.

1494
00:36:56,650 --> 00:36:58,210
So how does GLOMAP differ?

1495
00:36:58,410 --> 00:37:01,930
GLOMAP abandons the sequential foundation entirely.

1496
00:37:02,130 --> 00:37:04,250
It does not initialize a starting pair.

1497
00:37:04,450 --> 00:37:07,090
It does not build the model one camera at a time.

1498
00:37:07,290 --> 00:37:09,930
It attempts the incredibly audacious task

1499
00:37:10,130 --> 00:37:14,810
of solving the position of every single camera in the entire dataset simultaneously.

1500
00:37:15,010 --> 00:37:17,330
Wait, how is that mathematically possible?

1501
00:37:17,530 --> 00:37:21,690
If you throw a thousand unstructured ground images into the void all at once,

1502
00:37:21,890 --> 00:37:26,290
how can the software possibly organize them without a step by step baseline?

1503
00:37:26,490 --> 00:37:28,170
It splits the pose estimation into two

1504
00:37:28,370 --> 00:37:32,250
distinct phases, relying heavily on a concept called rotation averaging.

1505
00:37:32,450 --> 00:37:34,050
OK, rotation averaging.

1506
00:37:34,050 --> 00:37:37,450
Now we still run the initial feature extraction and correspondence search.

1507
00:37:37,650 --> 00:37:41,370
We still need that verified web of 2D matches connecting the images.

1508
00:37:41,370 --> 00:37:43,250
Right. So we know what photos share features.

1509
00:37:43,250 --> 00:37:46,050
We just don't know where the photos belong in physical 3D space.

1510
00:37:46,250 --> 00:37:49,730
Exactly. But instead of trying to find the XYZ location right away,

1511
00:37:49,930 --> 00:37:51,730
GLOMAP looks at everything in the 3D space.

1512
00:37:51,930 --> 00:37:54,970
It calculates every single pair of ratched images and performs a rapid

1513
00:37:55,170 --> 00:37:58,210
calculation to estimate only the relative rotation between them.

1514
00:37:58,410 --> 00:38:00,330
It ignores physical distance entirely.

1515
00:38:00,530 --> 00:38:02,330
Completely ignores distance.

1516
00:38:02,530 --> 00:38:06,130
It simply asks, how much did the photographer tilt or pan

1517
00:38:06,330 --> 00:38:08,650
the lens between photo A and photo B?

1518
00:38:08,770 --> 00:38:11,210
So it only cares about the direction the camera was pointing.

1519
00:38:11,410 --> 00:38:15,130
It calculates thousands of these isolated rotational guesses.

1520
00:38:15,330 --> 00:38:16,890
Then it throws all those relative

1521
00:38:17,090 --> 00:38:20,890
rotations into a massive global optimization algorithm.

1522
00:38:21,050 --> 00:38:23,650
The software searches for a single unified

1523
00:38:23,850 --> 00:38:28,650
orientation framework that satisfies as many of those individual guesses as possible.

1524
00:38:28,850 --> 00:38:32,210
Oh, I see. And because it is only solving for rotation,

1525
00:38:32,410 --> 00:38:36,090
the math is significantly less complex than a full bundle adjustment.

1526
00:38:36,290 --> 00:38:38,850
And it can actually leverage much more parallel processing.

1527
00:38:39,050 --> 00:38:41,170
Exactly. It gets every single camera pointing

1528
00:38:41,170 --> 00:38:45,090
in the correct direction relative to each other all at the exact same time.

1529
00:38:45,290 --> 00:38:48,290
But they are still just floating haphazardly in space.

1530
00:38:48,490 --> 00:38:50,210
The actual geometry doesn't exist yet.

1531
00:38:50,210 --> 00:38:54,490
Once the global rotation is locked, it initiates the global positioning step.

1532
00:38:54,690 --> 00:38:56,650
The algorithm slides all those properly

1533
00:38:56,650 --> 00:39:00,090
oriented cameras along their mathematical viewing rays, shifting them through the

1534
00:39:00,290 --> 00:39:03,890
void until the thousands of 2D feature matches all converge and intersect

1535
00:39:04,090 --> 00:39:05,730
on cohesive 3D points in the center.

1536
00:39:05,930 --> 00:39:10,610
It pulls the entire chaotic web tight in one massive movement.

1537
00:39:10,810 --> 00:39:14,370
The model basically pops into existence almost instantly.

1538
00:39:14,570 --> 00:39:16,690
The source notes that Geolab operates at

1539
00:39:16,890 --> 00:39:20,170
speeds that make incremental reconstruction look archaic.

1540
00:39:20,370 --> 00:39:24,490
But a shortcut that aggressive obviously comes with severe limitations.

1541
00:39:24,690 --> 00:39:25,770
Oh, absolutely.

1542
00:39:25,970 --> 00:39:28,010
When we look at our specific mission today,

1543
00:39:28,210 --> 00:39:33,810
capturing large outdoor spaces from the ground, Geolab is a high risk, high reward gamble.

1544
00:39:34,010 --> 00:39:35,090
Why is it high risk?

1545
00:39:35,290 --> 00:39:39,130
It requires an incredibly specific type of data collection to succeed.

1546
00:39:39,330 --> 00:39:42,290
Geolab demands a highly interconnected data set.

1547
00:39:42,490 --> 00:39:44,090
It thrives on loop closures.

1548
00:39:44,290 --> 00:39:46,650
Loop closures. Let's go back to the fountain example.

1549
00:39:46,850 --> 00:39:49,770
The videographer walked a complete circle around the structure, returning

1550
00:39:50,370 --> 00:39:52,170
a the exact patch of sidewalk where they started.

1551
00:39:52,370 --> 00:39:53,410
That is a loop closure, right?

1552
00:39:53,410 --> 00:39:53,690
Yes.

1553
00:39:53,890 --> 00:39:55,370
Because the end of the image sequence

1554
00:39:55,370 --> 00:39:57,650
physically overlaps with the beginning of the sequence,

1555
00:39:57,850 --> 00:40:02,050
the 2D matching web is incredibly dense and structurally found.

1556
00:40:02,250 --> 00:40:04,250
Geolab uses the strength of that closed

1557
00:40:04,450 --> 00:40:08,970
loop to anchor the global rotation map, pulling the entire fountain into perfect focus.

1558
00:40:09,170 --> 00:40:11,610
But consider a different outdoor capture.

1559
00:40:11,810 --> 00:40:15,770
Let's say I want to 3D model the storefronts on a historic main street.

1560
00:40:15,970 --> 00:40:19,370
I hold my camera and I walk straight down the sidewalk for 10 blocks,

1561
00:40:19,370 --> 00:40:22,130
capturing the facades as I go, and then I just stop.

1562
00:40:22,330 --> 00:40:23,930
I never turn around. I never walk back.

1563
00:40:23,930 --> 00:40:24,730
I never close the loop.

1564
00:40:24,930 --> 00:40:28,170
Straight line capture creates a brittle mathematical chain.

1565
00:40:28,370 --> 00:40:30,410
Frame one shares features with frame five.

1566
00:40:30,610 --> 00:40:32,130
Frame five connects to frame ten.

1567
00:40:32,330 --> 00:40:34,330
But frame one has absolutely zero

1568
00:40:34,530 --> 00:40:38,490
mathematical relationship to frame 1000 at the far end of the block.

1569
00:40:38,690 --> 00:40:40,650
The global web is incredibly weak.

1570
00:40:40,690 --> 00:40:42,650
If you feed that straight line data set

1571
00:40:42,650 --> 00:40:45,930
into Geolab, the global positioning math just collapses, doesn't it?

1572
00:40:45,930 --> 00:40:47,330
It cannot resolve the tension.

1573
00:40:47,530 --> 00:40:49,330
And the source describes the failure

1574
00:40:49,530 --> 00:40:52,090
of the state of Geolab and it is fascinatingly chaotic.

1575
00:40:52,290 --> 00:40:53,610
It's hilarious, honestly.

1576
00:40:53,810 --> 00:40:57,770
When incremental Geolab fails, it just slowly drifts and bends like a banana.

1577
00:40:57,970 --> 00:40:59,930
When Geolab fails, it panics.

1578
00:41:00,130 --> 00:41:01,530
The visual output is bizarre.

1579
00:41:01,730 --> 00:41:05,210
The source specifically refers to the failures as Borg cubes.

1580
00:41:05,410 --> 00:41:09,850
Borg cubes. Because the software lacks the loop closures to anchor the structure,

1581
00:41:10,050 --> 00:41:13,810
the cameras essentially pull each other into a localized gravitational collapse.

1582
00:41:14,010 --> 00:41:18,530
So instead of a smooth street, the software outputs a dense, glitchy,

1583
00:41:18,690 --> 00:41:23,370
perfectly square cube of overlapping cameras just hovering in empty space.

1584
00:41:23,570 --> 00:41:25,890
The geometry completely destroys itself.

1585
00:41:26,090 --> 00:41:29,010
The model implodes into a singularity.

1586
00:41:29,210 --> 00:41:33,650
But, you know, from a workflow perspective, even that catastrophic failure is highly valuable.

1587
00:41:33,850 --> 00:41:35,170
Because it fails fast.

1588
00:41:35,370 --> 00:41:40,010
You don't wait five hours for a master detective CPU to slowly build a warped,

1589
00:41:40,210 --> 00:41:44,210
unusable street. Geolab hands you a Borg cube in five minutes.

1590
00:41:44,210 --> 00:41:47,810
Right. You instantly know your data set lacks structural integrity.

1591
00:41:47,810 --> 00:41:50,010
So you switch your software back to the slow,

1592
00:41:50,210 --> 00:41:53,730
incremental car map and you let the CPU brute force the straight line.

1593
00:41:53,930 --> 00:41:56,050
Having both tools allows you to attack

1594
00:41:56,050 --> 00:41:59,370
different types of geometry with the appropriate mathematical strategy.

1595
00:41:59,570 --> 00:42:02,330
This is amazing. We have completely demystified the black box.

1596
00:42:02,330 --> 00:42:03,170
You really have.

1597
00:42:03,370 --> 00:42:06,890
We started with the goal of capturing a massive outdoor space without relying

1598
00:42:07,090 --> 00:42:10,930
on the cheat codes of a drone, and we examined how the SIFT algorithm acts

1599
00:42:10,930 --> 00:42:14,130
as the eyes of the pipeline, utilizing difference of the Gaussian's

1600
00:42:14,330 --> 00:42:17,770
to hunt for high contrast scale invariant freckles on the face of the architect.

1601
00:42:17,970 --> 00:42:21,410
Sure. We saw how to blind the software to dynamic

1602
00:42:21,610 --> 00:42:25,890
noise using masks and how to unbend the physical distortion of our lenses.

1603
00:42:25,890 --> 00:42:30,130
By defining the camera model, we explored how sequential and vocab tree matching

1604
00:42:30,330 --> 00:42:34,290
allow us to connect those features without crushing our hardware with N squared

1605
00:42:34,490 --> 00:42:38,730
exhaustive math. We verify the physics of those matches using homographies,

1606
00:42:38,930 --> 00:42:43,610
initialized our models by leveraging the parallax of a wide baseline and watch

1607
00:42:43,810 --> 00:42:47,770
the CPU act as a master detective to incrementally bundle, adjust the

1608
00:42:47,970 --> 00:42:49,090
geometry into reality.

1609
00:42:49,290 --> 00:42:53,170
And we looked at the bleeding edge, utilizing gelo map to instantly snap

1610
00:42:53,370 --> 00:42:57,330
a model together, provided we give it the dense loop closures it craves.

1611
00:42:57,530 --> 00:43:00,930
The theory is profound, but the source material ends with a direct

1612
00:43:01,130 --> 00:43:05,050
call to action, and it is one we definitely must echo to you, the listener.

1613
00:43:05,090 --> 00:43:07,450
Absolutely. Do not leave this knowledge in the abstract.

1614
00:43:07,650 --> 00:43:11,410
Reading about photogrammetry is vastly different from actually executing it.

1615
00:43:11,610 --> 00:43:17,570
Do not just download a pristine, prepackaged, open source data set of a statue where you know the lighting is perfect

1616
00:43:17,970 --> 00:43:19,970
and the model map is guaranteed to solve.

1617
00:43:20,170 --> 00:43:22,650
You have the necessary hardware in your pocket right now.

1618
00:43:22,850 --> 00:43:28,170
Go outside. Find a textured brick building, a local monument or a park fountain.

1619
00:43:28,370 --> 00:43:29,450
Grab your smartphone.

1620
00:43:29,650 --> 00:43:34,450
Walk around the structure, consciously thinking about overlap, texture and parallax.

1621
00:43:34,650 --> 00:43:35,530
Download CalmApp.

1622
00:43:35,730 --> 00:43:39,490
It is entirely free and open source and feed your own photos into the pipeline.

1623
00:43:39,690 --> 00:43:42,450
Watch the feature extraction isolate the details.

1624
00:43:42,650 --> 00:43:44,690
Watch the point cloud slowly build.

1625
00:43:44,890 --> 00:43:46,890
When your model inevitably collapses

1626
00:43:47,090 --> 00:43:47,770
into a board cube,

1627
00:43:47,970 --> 00:43:49,930
interrogate your capture strategy.

1628
00:43:50,130 --> 00:43:52,050
Did you capture too much blank sky?

1629
00:43:52,250 --> 00:43:55,250
Did you break the chronological chain by filming a dog?

1630
00:43:55,450 --> 00:43:59,250
The trial and error fundamentally changes how you perceive physical space.

1631
00:43:59,450 --> 00:44:04,250
You will never look at a blank white wall or a heavily textured stone carving the same way again.

1632
00:44:04,450 --> 00:44:05,970
It really rewires your brain.

1633
00:44:06,170 --> 00:44:10,770
It does. Now I want to leave you with a final lingering thought that builds on this entire discussion.

1634
00:44:10,970 --> 00:44:13,010
The core strength of Schoenberger's collection

1635
00:44:13,210 --> 00:44:17,090
mapper is that it does not need a drone, it does not need a top down grid,

1636
00:44:17,250 --> 00:44:19,650
and it does not need RTK GPS.

1637
00:44:19,850 --> 00:44:25,130
It was explicitly designed to handle the messy, random, overlapping reality of human photography.

1638
00:44:25,330 --> 00:44:27,690
It thrives on unstructured collections.

1639
00:44:27,890 --> 00:44:31,570
So consider the implications of scaling that capability.

1640
00:44:31,770 --> 00:44:35,890
What happens if we take this software and instead of feeding it a thousand photos

1641
00:44:36,090 --> 00:44:40,130
of a fountain taken this afternoon, we feed it millions of random historical

1642
00:44:40,330 --> 00:44:42,930
tourist photos of a city center from the 1980s.

1643
00:44:43,130 --> 00:44:46,250
Oh, wow. Millions of physical photos pulled from attics,

1644
00:44:46,250 --> 00:44:49,530
scanned into digital files, completely unstructured,

1645
00:44:49,730 --> 00:44:54,090
but overlapping by sheer coincidence because thousands of different tourists

1646
00:44:54,290 --> 00:44:57,010
stood in roughly the same spots over the course of a decade.

1647
00:44:57,210 --> 00:45:00,850
I mean, the SIFT algorithm does not care if the photos were taken ten years apart.

1648
00:45:01,050 --> 00:45:05,890
If the structural contrast of the stone remains static, the software will match the features.

1649
00:45:06,090 --> 00:45:06,810
Exactly.

1650
00:45:07,010 --> 00:45:11,210
We are standing on the verge of being able to dynamically 3D reconstruct the past.

1651
00:45:11,410 --> 00:45:16,090
We could build fully nautical photorealistic geometric environments of monuments

1652
00:45:16,290 --> 00:45:19,730
that have long been destroyed or city squares that have been paved over,

1653
00:45:19,930 --> 00:45:25,090
utilizing absolutely nothing but the accidental collective 2D memories sitting in our photo albums.

1654
00:45:25,290 --> 00:45:26,930
That is wild to think about.

1655
00:45:27,130 --> 00:45:29,610
We can cure the dimensional blindness of history.

1656
00:45:29,810 --> 00:45:32,010
Something to chew on. That is a deep dive.

